00:35.370 --> 00:37.300
So what's the average
value of a function?

00:37.300 --> 00:39.180
And you learned
this in calculus.

00:39.180 --> 00:40.660
You might not remember it.

00:43.640 --> 00:50.020
So let's say the average value
of f is equal to essentially--

00:50.020 --> 00:53.860
let's say, between 2 points, b
and a-- it's the difference

00:53.860 --> 00:57.230
between the 2 points divided by
the area under the

00:57.230 --> 01:00.780
curve of f of x.

01:00.780 --> 01:01.840
From a to b.

01:01.840 --> 01:02.190
Right?

01:02.190 --> 01:04.470
That's, I think they call
that the second fundamental

01:04.470 --> 01:05.300
theorem of calculus.

01:05.300 --> 01:08.180
Or corollary to the fundamental
theorem of calculus, whatever.

01:08.180 --> 01:09.140
But it makes sense.

01:09.140 --> 01:09.700
Right?

01:09.700 --> 01:13.790
The average value of a function
is equal to the area under the

01:13.790 --> 01:16.210
curve divided by, you
could kind of say, the

01:16.210 --> 01:17.350
base of the figure.

01:17.350 --> 01:18.260
Right?

01:18.260 --> 01:20.820
Because another way to think
about it, if you just took the

01:20.820 --> 01:26.930
average value of the function--
so if the function was, say,

01:26.930 --> 01:31.310
this, and you just multiplied
that average value times the

01:31.310 --> 01:34.660
base, you would get the
same area as the integral.

01:34.660 --> 01:35.550
That's another way to view it.

01:35.550 --> 01:39.460
You could rewrite
this as b minus a.

01:39.460 --> 01:42.540
The base times the average
value is the same thing as

01:42.540 --> 01:43.655
the area under the curve.

01:47.010 --> 01:48.490
So that's another
way to view it.

01:48.490 --> 01:49.670
So let's just apply that here.

01:49.670 --> 01:53.670
Let's use the trapezoidal sum
to figure out the area under

01:53.670 --> 01:57.840
this curve, or an estimate of
the area under l of t, and then

01:57.840 --> 01:59.750
we can divide by b minus a.

01:59.750 --> 02:02.810
Or in that case,
